# UX / UI behavior

Do not copy Material’s look unless the user asked for “like Google”. Take the **behavior** ([Material 3 Interaction](https://m3.material.io/foundations), Nielsen heuristics).

- System status is visible: request, success, error, place in nav (`aria-current`). Do not stay silent after a click.
- Every interactive has the full set: default, hover (where a mouse exists), focus, **pressed**, disabled (+ dragged if it is dragged). Pressed is visible on the press frame, including on phones. Not color alone — also light/layer/outline/scale (state layer).
- Touch target: 24px minimum (WCAG), **48×48** Material target. Space between dangerous buttons.
- One primary CTA per screen. Secondary is weaker. Not three equally loud buttons in one block.
- The same things look and behave the same on every page (button, back, search, help).
- The person stays in control: back, cancel, Escape, undo after danger. Do not steal the OS back gesture with your swipe.
- Recognition over recall: labels on buttons, no nameless icons; hints before errors.
- Motion explains where a layer came from (modal grows from the action, no teleport). No motion if `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- What is on top is more important: modal above the page, toast does not cover the main CTA. Not a flat “same z everywhere”.
- Errors: do not blame; say how to fix. Destructive — confirm. Empty and 404 — a path forward.
- Scan path: heading → point → action. Do not hide price or submit three screens down without a reason.
- Do not require sign-up before value unless the user asked for that.
